WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.700
- [Narrator] Widespread floating trains.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.320
Nuclear fusion.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.238
Marty McFly's hoverboard.

00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:07.440
- He's on a hoverboard.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.300
- [Narrator] It sounds like the future

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.190
but many scientists say these types

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:14.220
of advances aren't as
far away as we think.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.000
They all hinge on one
thing, a room-temperature,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.390
ambient pressure
superconductor, and in July,

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.330
some scientists claimed
to have found just that,

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:25.950
sending stock prices for companies

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.430
with a perceived connection
to superconductors soaring.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:30.540
- A breakthrough like this

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.930
really could mean longer lasting
batteries for our devices,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:35.850
revolutionizing medical imaging.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.220
It could mean more
efficient electrical grids.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.890
It could mean improved high speed trains.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.360
There's almost no aspect

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.450
of our daily life that a room-temperature,

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.700
ambient pressure
superconductor wouldn't touch

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.200
if commercially applied.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.460
- [Narrator] That's why investors,

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.350
despite doubts surrounding the search,

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.300
are still backing the effort.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.770
Here's where the science stands

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:56.603
and what it could take

00:00:56.603 --> 00:00:58.713
to bring such a superconductor to market.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.280
When electricity flows
from one place to another,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.300
it travels along some type of
conductor, usually a metal.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.240
Those conductors put up some
resistance, which results

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.970
in some of the energy being lost as heat.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.810
That's why your phone or
computer can get really warm.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.240
Superconductors eliminate that resistance

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.850
and can transmit energy without loss.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.290
- Superconductors, obviously,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.510
if they conduct electric
current perfectly,

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.490
it means they're extremely efficient.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.980
- [Narrator] But these
materials typically only work

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:30.600
in extreme conditions,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.920
temperatures as cold

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.220
as roughly negative 320 degrees Fahrenheit

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.500
or extremely high pressure.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:38.610
- There's always an investment

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.740
to do the necessary cryogenics.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:44.640
If you're using copper and
it has a little bit of loss

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.430
but the superconductor
is much more expensive

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.510
for most of your applications,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.100
you might wanna use copper instead.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.780
Just because you have a superconductor,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.850
the question is whether
it's gonna be useful or not.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:54.690
- [Narrator] Because of that,

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.750
superconductors, as they
exist today, are used

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:01.680
in limited circumstances,
for example, MRI machines

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:04.860
but there's also been
investment in broader uses.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.290
The Departments of Energy
and Homeland Security

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:10.590
have funded initiatives to
create more resilient power grids

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:12.930
using superconducting cables, like one

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:16.920
in Chicago that uses liquid
nitrogen to keep cables cool.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.830
- Ultimately, the reason
why people are so interested

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.140
in this space in terms
of investment especially

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.540
is because unlocking a room-temperature

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.700
and pressure superconductor again,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.010
would revolutionize medical imaging.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.710
It would make it so that
magnetically levitating trains

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.890
were more accessible, cheaper
to build, more efficient.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:38.250
It would make it so that
our electrical grids

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.020
truly would become revolutionized.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.970
- [Narrator] Because
superconductors potentially

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.220
promise huge advances in technology,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.680
finding one that can
work at room-temperature

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.410
and ambient pressure
has become a holy grail

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.050
for scientists and investors.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:55.110
A group led by scientists at
the University of Rochester

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.660
claimed to make a major
breakthrough in March.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.810
A superconductor made of
rare-earth material combined

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.180
with nitrogen and hydrogen
that they said worked

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.430
at roughly room temperature.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.020
The study was peer reviewed

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.170
but the lead researcher
has come under scrutiny.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.150
The University of Rochester-led group

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.820
did not respond to requests for comment.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.520
Then two other papers released in July

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.560
detailed a potential superconductor

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.010
called LK-99 that combined copper, lead,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:24.510
phosphorus and oxygen

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.900
and worked at room temperature
and ambient pressure.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.510
Videos of the substance
floating above a magnet,

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.080
a hallmark of superconductivity,
circulated online.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:37.140
But those papers, written by
researchers who mostly worked

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.140
for the privately held Quantum
Energy Research Center,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.120
haven't yet been peer reviewed

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.080
and many scientists say
LK-99 is just a magnet

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.720
rather than a superconducting material.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.200
- Many groups had difficulty
replicating the experiment.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.970
- [Narrator] The Quantum
Energy Research Center

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.370
didn't respond to requests for comment.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.130
Still, the enthusiasm
for the July research

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:02.340
triggered big stock swings
for companies like SuNAM,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.980
Mobiis and American Superconductor.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:08.160
Before a room-temperature,
ambient pressure superconductor

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.660
can be brought to market,

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.870
it needs to be replicated
regularly in trials.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:13.950
That's what Norman's group

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.630
at Argonne National Laboratory

00:04:15.630 --> 00:04:18.330
is trying to do now with LK-99.

00:04:18.330 --> 00:04:20.310
- It's gonna sort of
take a while to sort out

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.720
what's actually going on there.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.280
It opens up the space, right?

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.140
There's lots and lots
of minerals out there,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.440
very few that have been studied
from a physics perspective.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.440
Maybe there are other surprises
out there waiting for us.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.290
- [Narrator] Replicating results
can take weeks or months,

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.580
if even possible.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.710
Then its production needs to be scaled up,

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.200
which can take much longer.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.390
- It's one thing having
little microscopic samples

00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:44.670
in your laboratory, it's another thing

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.750
to make kilometer-long wires.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:49.583
We just don't know.

00:04:49.583 --> 00:04:51.270
Until you know what the material is

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.130
and how easy it is to make,

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:56.250
and then it's like how can you wrap it

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.780
and make a wire out of it?

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.070
Until you know those things,
it's really hard to figure out

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.700
what kind of lag will be from
discovery to applications.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.560
- [Narrator] The superconductor
cable system used

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:09.990
for the power grid in Chicago took years

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:13.500
and approximately $19
million to implement.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.660
- Any discovery or invention or change

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.360
in the field is ultimately net positive.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:22.050
So it's almost like a nothing to lose,

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.030
everything to gain scenario.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:27.210
It's not like they have spent
decades researching this,

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:31.170
trying new recipes, and
it has yielded no results.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:34.918
So it's perhaps just a matter of time.

00:05:34.918 --> 00:05:37.585
(pensive music)

